The code statements that lead to the bug are:
unsigned int claimed:1;
unsigned int can_retune:1;
unsigned int retune_now:1;
unsigned int retune_paused:1;
Reason:
These bitfields share the same underlying word, so a read-modify-write operation on one field (e.g., `claimed` in `__mmc_claim_host()`) can overwrite concurrent updates to other fields (e.g., `retune_now` in `mmc_mq_queue_rq()`). This causes corruption and spurious warnings like `WARN_ON(!host->claimed)`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
bool claimed;
bool can_retune;
bool retune_now;
bool retune_paused;
Reason:
Converting these fields from bitfields to separate `bool` variables eliminates the shared-word coupling. Each field occupies its own memory location, preventing unrelated read-modify-write side effects in asynchronous contexts.

